Three cheap ALWAYS controls for `forge serve` on a multi-user box, plus the
B5a security-model doc-drift fix.

- serve.lock single-instance guard: exclusive-create lock with dead-PID stale
  reclaim + clean release on SIGINT/SIGTERM/exit — a second `forge serve` for
  the same project can no longer silently port-squat.
- securePath(): lock/journal created 0o600 and their dir 0o700 at creation,
  plus a loud startup audit for group/other-readable sensitive paths. Windows
  chmod is a documented best-effort no-op (NTFS ACLs, not POSIX mode bits); the
  audit prints an honest caveat instead of a false alarm.
- Hash-chained mutation journal (.forge/serve/journal.jsonl): every mutation
  attempt carries sha256(prevHash || record); verifyJournal() detects any edited
  or deleted past record. Wired into handleMutation (best-effort, never fails a
  response).
- New authoritative docs/work/2026-07-13-forge-serve/security-model.md: endpoint
  auth matrix showing GET /data.json is token-gated (corrects the stale
  "unauthenticated gap"), plus the by-design note that local actor/origin is
  advisory coordination metadata, never authenticated.

State lives under .forge/serve/ so the shared .forge/ config dir is never
re-chmod'd. Kernel remains the sole mutation authority — forward-compatible with
the hosted/sync tier.

Issues: 8eabd92c (serve hardening), d3c47131 (B5a doc-drift).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
